1
00:00:00,134 --> 00:00:05,245
Most satisfaction surveys lose people at
the click-through: open a link, wait for a

2
00:00:05,245 --> 00:00:06,907
page, then fill out a form.

3
00:00:06,987 --> 00:00:11,836
With Klick-Zen, the rating buttons sit
right in the email, so a single click is

4
00:00:11,836 --> 00:00:12,941
the entire survey.

5
00:00:13,021 --> 00:00:18,813
Start with a button for happy customers.
In Klick-Zen, click Create new button, and

6
00:00:18,813 --> 00:00:20,069
name it CSAT Good.

7
00:00:20,149 --> 00:00:25,286
Type its label right on the button: Great.
Then give it a friendly green.

8
00:00:25,366 --> 00:00:31,371
Now the actions. Click Add button action,
choose Add Ticket Tag, and enter

9
00:00:31,371 --> 00:00:32,183
csat_good.

10
00:00:32,263 --> 00:00:39,804
Add a second action, Update Ticket Field,
to set a csat_rating custom field to good.

11
00:00:39,804 --> 00:00:41,420
Then click Create.

12
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,891
Then build its partner, Not so good. It
adds the tag csat_bad, and sets

13
00:00:46,891 --> 00:00:48,334
csat_rating to bad.

14
00:00:48,414 --> 00:00:54,146
It also sets the ticket status back to
Open, so an unhappy customer gets

15
00:00:54,146 --> 00:00:57,171
attention straight away. Click Create.

16
00:00:57,251 --> 00:01:01,139
That's the simple version: two buttons,
good or bad.

17
00:01:01,219 --> 00:01:07,274
Want more detail? Build five buttons
instead, from 1, Very bad, to 5, Great,

18
00:01:07,274 --> 00:01:09,903
each with its own rating and tag.

19
00:01:09,983 --> 00:01:14,352
Put the buttons in the email that goes out
when a ticket is solved. That can be a

20
00:01:14,352 --> 00:01:16,671
trigger email, or part of your solve
macro.

21
00:01:16,751 --> 00:01:21,355
Write a short question, then paste the two
snippets side by side.

22
00:01:21,435 --> 00:01:24,773
Save the trigger, and your survey is live.

23
00:01:24,853 --> 00:01:28,404
Your customer gets one simple question,
and two buttons.

24
00:01:28,484 --> 00:01:33,223
One click on Great, and they land on your
branded thank-you page. That's the whole

25
00:01:33,223 --> 00:01:33,628
survey.

26
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:38,972
Back in Zendesk, the rating is already on
the ticket, as a tag and a custom field,

27
00:01:38,972 --> 00:01:41,411
ready to report on in Zendesk Explore.

28
00:01:41,491 --> 00:01:46,620
Here's where it gets powerful. Because the
rating is a tag, your Zendesk triggers can

29
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:47,827
act on it instantly.

30
00:01:47,907 --> 00:01:52,861
When csat_bad is added, a trigger can
reopen the ticket, raise the priority to

31
00:01:52,861 --> 00:01:57,371
High, alert a team lead, and tell the
customer a senior agent is on it.

32
00:01:57,451 --> 00:02:02,380
A poor rating stops being a number in a
dashboard, and becomes a recovery that

33
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:03,770
starts within seconds.

34
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:08,512
Compared with Zendesk's built-in survey,
you choose the wording, the look, the

35
00:02:08,512 --> 00:02:10,185
scale, and where it appears.

36
00:02:10,265 --> 00:02:15,186
Use it in any trigger, macro, or reply,
even where native satisfaction surveys

37
00:02:15,186 --> 00:02:16,259
aren't available.

38
00:02:16,339 --> 00:02:21,203
Find Klick-Zen on the Zendesk Marketplace,
and make your next survey a single click.
